# Python modules and imports

- Define a private helper on the class when it implements that class's behavior and only that class uses it. Keep shared helpers and independent computations at module scope.
- Prefer the public package re-export when a symbol is available there.
- When a re-export introduces an import cycle, import directly from the defining submodule. Avoid relying on import order to mask a cycle.

Why: keeping class-specific helpers with their class makes ownership clear. Package re-exports give callers a stable public API. Import order can hide a cycle until a formatter or another caller changes the order.

